# Episode 4: HMS Victoria, 1893. (shot, chapter-or-None, narration)
SCRIPT = [
 ("fleet", None, "On a calm summer afternoon in eighteen ninety-three, the most powerful fleet in the world was steaming off the coast of what's now Lebanon. Eleven British ironclads, in two neat lines. The weather was perfect. There was no enemy for hundreds of miles."),
 ("collision", None, "Less than an hour later, the fleet's flagship was at the bottom of the sea, along with its admiral and three hundred and fifty-eight of its crew. It had been rammed by another British battleship. On the admiral's own orders."),
 ("title", None, "This is the story of HMS Victoria. The day the Royal Navy sank its own flagship, and nobody on the bridge felt able to stop it."),

 ("fleet", "The Pride of the Fleet", "In the eighteen nineties, Britain's Royal Navy was the largest and most powerful navy on Earth. And one of its most important commands was the Mediterranean Fleet, guarding the sea routes that linked Britain to India and the rest of its empire."),
 ("victoria", None, "Its flagship was HMS Victoria. She was a strange-looking ship. At the front sat a single huge turret, carrying two enormous sixteen-and-a-quarter-inch guns. Her foredeck was so low that in even a slight sea it disappeared under the waves. Sailors nicknamed her the Slipper."),
 ("victoria", None, "She'd already had one embarrassing accident. In January eighteen ninety-two, the Victoria ran aground near Platea in Greece, and had to be refloated and repaired in Malta. That was bad luck. What happened next was not."),
 ("tryon", None, "Commanding the fleet was Vice-Admiral Sir George Tryon. Many of his contemporaries saw him as brilliant. A supremely competent, radical thinker, who believed a fleet should be able to manoeuvre quickly, with as few signals as possible."),
 ("signals", None, "Tryon even proposed a simpler way of signalling. Two flags, T and A, would tell captains to simply follow their leader, instead of relying on the huge official signal book. The Times called the idea unsound in theory and perilous in practice. Others thought it was exactly what a modern fleet needed."),
 ("tryon", None, "He was also known for his manner. One magazine later described him as a very brusque and dictatorial man. Officers who served under Tryon learned that you didn't question the admiral lightly."),
 ("tryon", None, "Ironically, Tryon had written about exactly that problem. In a memorandum to his commanders, he told them that their first duty was to keep their ship safe. If an order looked dangerous, they should try to achieve its intent, but only if it could be done without risk to their ship or others."),

 ("fleet", "The Order", "On the twenty-second of June, eighteen ninety-three, the fleet was carrying out its summer exercises, about five miles off the coast of Tripoli, then part of the Ottoman Empire. Tryon's ship, the Victoria, led one line of six ships. The second line of five was led by HMS Camperdown, carrying his second-in-command, Rear-Admiral Albert Hastings Markham."),
 ("diagram_a", None, "The two lines were steaming side by side, six cables apart. That's twelve hundred yards. Tryon now planned a showy manoeuvre for the approach to the anchorage. Both lines would turn inwards, towards each other, and reverse direction, so the fleet came in heading the other way."),
 ("diagram_a", None, "There was just one problem. Every one of those ships needed space to turn. Each battleship's turning circle was around eight hundred yards across. Two ships turning towards each other needed at least sixteen hundred yards between them to pass safely. The fleet had twelve hundred."),
 ("bridge", None, "Tryon's officers saw it. According to reports at the time, his staff commander, Thomas Hawkins-Smith, advised that the lines should be eight cables apart, and the admiral seemed to agree. But the signal that went up was for six. When this was pointed out, Tryon reportedly replied: let it remain."),
 ("bridge", None, "His flag captain, Maurice Bourke, later admitted under pressure that he had reminded the admiral of Victoria's turning circle. Tryon held to his order. Nobody on Victoria's bridge pushed any harder than that."),

 ("signals", "What Are You Waiting For?", "The signal flags went up. Both lines were to turn sixteen points, a full half-circle, inwards. Aboard the Camperdown, Rear-Admiral Markham stared at the order. He could see that it made no sense."),
 ("signals", None, "So Markham hesitated. He didn't repeat the signal, which meant he wasn't ready to obey. And then, from the flagship, came a semaphore message, asking what he was waiting for."),
 ("signals", None, "It was a public rebuke from the commander-in-chief, in front of the entire fleet. Markham convinced himself that Tryon must have a plan he couldn't see. Perhaps the admiral meant to swing around the outside of Markham's line. Either way, he gave in. The Camperdown began to turn."),

 ("diagram_b", "Collision", "At around three thirty in the afternoon, the two flagships swung towards each other. On both bridges, officers watched the gap closing far too fast. The Camperdown's engines were put astern. The Victoria's captain reversed his screws too. It was too late."),
 ("collision", None, "Warships of this era were built with a strange weapon in mind. A ram. After an Austrian ironclad rammed and sank an Italian ship at the Battle of Lissa in eighteen sixty-six, navies around the world fitted their battleships with heavy, pointed bows below the waterline, designed to punch holes in enemy hulls."),
 ("collision", None, "Below the waterline, the Camperdown carried a heavy armoured ram, built to sink enemy ships. It struck the Victoria on her starboard bow and drove deep into her hull. Water flooded in. For a moment, the two ships were locked together. Then the Camperdown backed away, and the sea poured into the hole."),
 ("collision", None, "According to his flag lieutenant, Tryon said, it is all my fault."),

 ("sinking", "Fifteen Minutes", "The Victoria's bow began to go down. Her low foredeck, the one that had earned her that nickname, slipped under almost at once. Tryon reportedly tried to steer her towards the shore, hoping to beach her in shallow water. But she was sinking too quickly."),
 ("sinking", None, "Within about fifteen minutes, the Victoria rolled over and plunged, bow first, her stern rising high out of the water. Men in the sea were dragged down with her or struck by the wreckage. Three hundred and fifty-eight of her crew died. Admiral Tryon went down with his ship."),
 ("rescue", None, "Boats from the other ships pulled survivors from the water. Among them was Victoria's executive officer, a commander called John Jellicoe, who had been ill in bed with a fever when the collision happened. He survived. Remember that name."),

 ("paper", "The Court Martial", "Back in Britain, the news was met with disbelief. How could the finest fleet in the world sink its own flagship on a calm sunny day, with no enemy in sight? A relief fund was set up for the families of the men who died, and within weeks it had raised around fifty thousand pounds. And a court martial was called to find out what had happened."),
 ("court", None, "Technically, it was Victoria's surviving officers who were on trial, led by Captain Bourke. The court's verdict put the blame squarely on the turning signal issued by Admiral Tryon. Bourke and the other survivors were acquitted."),
 ("signals", None, "The captain of the Camperdown told the court he had been confident that turning in six cables would cause a collision, until Markham suggested the admiral must intend to circle around the outside of their line. Markham himself admitted he'd never seen a manoeuvre like it."),
 ("bridge", None, "Other evidence was just as damning. Staff Commander Hawkins-Smith said he thought the manoeuvre needed about ten cables. And Markham had told his own flag lieutenant that the movement ordered was impossible. He'd known. He just hadn't said so to the one person who mattered."),
 ("court", None, "Markham was a harder case. The court regretted that he had not asked for an explanation of the order. But it also declared that it would be fatal to the navy if junior officers started questioning their admiral's orders. In other words, he should have spoken up. But also, he shouldn't have."),
 ("paper", None, "The newspapers wrestled with the same contradiction. Many expressed regret that Markham had not dared to disobey the order, though few were surprised that he hadn't. The disaster raised a question every military has to answer. When is it right to disobey?"),

 ("ghost", None, "The disaster also produced one of the Victorian era's most famous ghost stories. Legend says that on the afternoon of the sinking, Lady Tryon was hosting a party at her home in London, when several guests saw the admiral, in full uniform, walk silently across the room. Of course, it's only a legend. But it's been retold ever since."),
 ("jellicoe", "What Remains", "John Jellicoe, the officer who survived with a fever, went on to command Britain's Grand Fleet during the First World War. In nineteen sixteen, he led it at the Battle of Jutland, the biggest naval battle of the war. He later became First Sea Lord, the professional head of the whole Royal Navy."),
 ("wreck", None, "As for the Victoria, she lay lost for more than a century. Then, on the twenty-second of August, two thousand and four, divers Christian Francis and Mark Ellyatt found her at a depth of about a hundred and forty metres. And she was in a very strange position."),
 ("wreck", None, "The Victoria is standing vertically on the seabed. Her bow and about thirty metres of her hull are buried in the mud, and her stern points straight up towards the surface. A giant warship, frozen in the moment of her final plunge."),
 ("fleet", None, "The sinking of HMS Victoria wasn't caused by a storm, an enemy, or a broken machine. It was caused by one confident man giving one bad order, and a room full of capable officers who didn't feel able to say no. Navies, airlines and hospitals still study that lesson today."),
 ("bridge", None, "Today, there's a name for this problem: the authority gradient. When the person in charge is too confident, and everyone else is too deferential, warnings don't get said out loud. Modern airline crews are trained specifically to challenge a captain who's making a mistake. In eighteen ninety-three, on the bridge of the Victoria, nobody had that training."),
 ("outro", None, "That was the loss of HMS Victoria. If you enjoyed it, subscribe to Blunderbuss History for the full stories of history's biggest blunders. See you in the next one."),
]
